In the free module on all final relator indices, $$\label{eq:translated-counts}
 \sum_{j=1}^{n_z}\sigma_{z,j}\bar u_{z,j}e_{r_{z,j}}
       =e_{\rho_z}.$$

There is a constant $C\ge1$, depending only on the finite choices above, with the following property on $\Gamma$ and on every finite quotient. Suppose that all $S$-labels have distance at most $\epsilon$ from $1$, and that all translates of the ordinary relators in $\mathcal A$ have product $1$. Put $$h=\sup_{z\in\mathcal D,\ v}|L(\rho_z,v)-1|,$$ where the supremum is $0$ if $\mathcal D$ is empty. If $0\le h,\epsilon\le1$, then, for every $z\in\mathcal D$ and vertex $v$, $$\begin{aligned}
 |L(z,v)-L(\rho_z,v)|&\le C(h+\epsilon)h,
                      \label{eq:quadratic-estimate}\\
 |L(z,v)-1|&\le C\epsilon.\label{eq:short-word-estimate}
\end{aligned}$$

The finite flag complex $L$, its nontrivial representation $\alpha:\pi_1(L)\to H$, and the group $G$ of <a href="#eq:height-kernel" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:height-kernel">[eq:height-kernel]</a> have the following properties.*

1.  *$G$ is countable, finitely generated, and residually finite, with $\operatorname{cd}_{\mathbb Z}(G)=2$ and $\operatorname{gd}(G)\le3$.*

2.  *$G$ acts freely on the integrally acyclic two-dimensional simplicial complex $X$ of <a href="#eq:zero-level" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:zero-level">[eq:zero-level]</a>. The action has finitely many cell orbits and one vertex orbit, identified with $G$ acting on itself on the left.*


3.  *There is a finite generating alphabet $S$ for $G$ and a finite indexed list $\mathcal D$ of words trivial in $G$ such that, for $\Lambda=\mathbb Z[G]$, the graph of $X$ has edges $g\to gs$ and $$\label{eq:model-resolution}
           0\longrightarrow\Lambda^{(\mathcal D)}
           \xrightarrow{\ \partial_2\ }\Lambda^{(S)}
           \xrightarrow{\ \partial_1\ }\Lambda
           \xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{aug}\ }\mathbb Z\longrightarrow0$$ is exact, with $\partial_1(e_s)=s-1$ and $\partial_2(e_z)=[z]$. Here $[z]$ is the oriented edge chain of the triangular boundary word $z$ based at $1$. In particular, the chains $[z]$, $z\in\mathcal D$, are a free $\Lambda$-basis of $\ker\partial_1$.
